      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Concrete ledger discrepancy: ECIWAG with multiplication by one returns an index level, incompatible with the registered percent_growth unit and quarterly-growth series name. The index observations above document this discrepancy and are not forecast anchors. The forecast retains the registered target identity and BLS printed percentage resolver; the raw-index adapter requires correction before it can automatically resolve that percentage."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: Use a blend of 60% ten-quarter reference-class mean and 40% latest-print persistence: 0.60*0.85 + 0.40*0.90 = 0.87%. Apply a judgmental momentum adjustment of -0.04 percentage points for weak September wage growth and hiring; one-off and policy-mechanism adjustments are zero. Point = 0.83%. Because this is a change series, estimate dispersion from the fetched quarterly growth values themselves: sum of squared deviations from 0.85 = 0.145; sigma = 0.12692955 percentage points = sqrt(0.145/9). The approximate normal 80% predictive half-width is 1.28*sigma = 0.16246983. Bounds = 0.83 +/- 0.16246983 = [0.66753017, 0.99246983], rounded to [0.67, 0.99]. No additional widening or narrowing is applied."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "An upside risk is broader contractual pay increases or persistent wage pressure producing a first print of 1.0% or more, outside the interval above 0.99%. A downside risk is sharper demand weakness and restrained pay settlements producing 0.6% or less, outside the interval below 0.67%. The small historical sample and imperfect hourly-earnings proxy limit interval calibration."
